\description{
Draws a marker carrying a text \code{label} (typically a rank or index) at each
observation. Without a \code{label} mapping, the \code{info} entries are numbered 1,
2, 3, ... in the order they appear in the data. Map \code{focus} to a logical variable to draw markers only for the
observations where it is \code{TRUE}; add \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_point]{ggplot2::geom_point()}} to show the
other observations as background. When \code{info} is mapped, a legend is added
that pairs each numbered marker with its description.

Map \code{label}, \code{focus} and \code{info} in \code{geom_focus()} rather than in
\code{\link[ggplot2:ggplot]{ggplot2::ggplot()}} when the plot has other layers. Layers inherit the
plot mapping, so their observations would also appear in the \code{info}
legend.

The markers use the standard \code{colour}, \code{fill}, \code{shape}, \code{size}, \code{stroke}
and \code{alpha} aesthetics, so the usual ggplot2 scales apply to them. Their
defaults come from the theme: a \code{paper} filled circle with an \code{ink} border
and label. A marker grows beyond \code{size} when its label needs more room.

Markers are drawn at the data positions and don't avoid each other. To
separate markers that overlap, map \code{nudge_x} or \code{nudge_y} and set
\code{position = "nudge"}.
}
